<com:TContent ID="body" >

<h1>Caching</h1>
<com:DocLink ClassPath="Prado.Caching.ICache" />

<p class="block-content">
A cache stores a value under a key so that later requests can read it back instead of
computing it again. PRADO uses one internally, for compiled templates and for
<a href="?page=Configurations.AppConfig">configuration</a> parsing, and an application uses
the same one for its own data.
</p>

<p class="block-content">
Caching is off until a cache module is declared. Declaring one is all the framework needs
to start using it for its own work.
</p>

<com:TTextHighlighter Language="xml" CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;modules&gt;
  &lt;module id="cache" class="Prado\Caching\TDbCache" /&gt;
&lt;/modules&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">The same configuration in the PHP format:</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="php" CssClass="source block-content">
'modules' => [
    'cache' => ['class' => 'Prado\Caching\TDbCache'],
],
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<h2>Reading and writing</h2>

<p class="block-content">
The <tt>Cache</tt> property of the application returns the cache module, or null when none
is configured, so code that may run either way checks first. Every cache module implements
the same five methods.
</p>

<table class="parameters">
<tr><th>Method</th><th>Does</th></tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>get($id)</tt></td>
	<td>Returns the value, or false when the key is absent or expired. A cached value of false is indistinguishable from a miss, so store something else.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>set($id, $value, $expire, $dependency)</tt></td>
	<td>Stores a value, replacing any existing one. <tt>$expire</tt> is seconds, and 0 means no expiry.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>add($id, $value, $expire, $dependency)</tt></td>
	<td>The same, except it does nothing when the key already exists. Use it when the first writer should win.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>delete($id)</tt></td>
	<td>Removes one key.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>flush()</tt></td>
	<td>Removes everything. A cache shared by more than one application loses the other application's entries too.</td>
</tr>
</table>

<com:TTextHighlighter Language="php" CssClass="source block-content">
$cache = $this-&gt;Application-&gt;Cache;
if ($cache !== null) {
    $rows = $cache-&gt;get('report.monthly');
    if ($rows === false) {
        $rows = $this-&gt;buildReport();
        $cache-&gt;set('report.monthly', $rows, 3600);
    }
}
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<h2>Keys and shared stores</h2>

<p class="block-content">
Every key is written with a prefix, so two applications sharing one store do not read each
other's values. <tt>KeyPrefix</tt> sets it, and it defaults to the application's unique id.
Set it explicitly when several applications must share entries, or when the same
application runs from more than one directory.
</p>

<p class="block-content">
<tt>PrimaryCache</tt> marks a module as the one the framework itself uses for templates and
configuration. It defaults to false. An application that declares more than one cache marks
exactly one as primary and reaches the others by module id.
</p>

<com:TTextHighlighter Language="php" CssClass="source block-content">
$sessions = $this-&gt;Application-&gt;getModule('sessioncache');
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<h2>The cache modules</h2>

<p class="block-content">
Five modules ship with the framework. They differ in where the data lives and in what the
server must provide.
</p>

<h3>TDbCache</h3>
<com:SinceVersion Version="3.1.0" />
<p class="block-content">
Stores entries in a database table. It needs no extension beyond PDO, which makes it the
one that works almost anywhere, and it survives a restart. <tt>ConnectionID</tt> names a
<a href="?page=Database.DAO">TDataSourceConfig</a> module, or the connection properties are
given directly. With neither, it creates a SQLite database in the runtime directory.
</p>

<table class="parameters">
<tr><th>Property</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
<tr><td><tt>ConnectionID</tt></td><td>The data source module holding the connection.</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>CacheTableName</tt></td><td>The table to use.</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>AutoCreateCacheTable</tt></td><td>Whether the table is created when missing.</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>FlushInterval</tt></td><td>How often expired rows are cleared out, in seconds.</td></tr>
</table>

<com:TTextHighlighter Language="xml" CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;modules&gt;
  &lt;module id="db" class="Prado\Data\TDataSourceConfig"&gt;
    &lt;database ConnectionString="mysql:host=localhost;dbname=mydb"
              Username="dbuser" Password="dbpass" /&gt;
  &lt;/module&gt;
  &lt;module id="cache" class="Prado\Caching\TDbCache"
          ConnectionID="db" CacheTableName="pradocache" /&gt;
&lt;/modules&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">The same configuration in the PHP format:</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="php" CssClass="source block-content">
'modules' => [
    'db' => [
        'class' => 'Prado\Data\TDataSourceConfig',
        'database' => [
            'ConnectionString' => 'mysql:host=localhost;dbname=mydb',
            'Username' => 'dbuser',
            'Password' => 'dbpass',
        ],
    ],
    'cache' => [
        'class' => 'Prado\Caching\TDbCache',
        'properties' => ['ConnectionID' => 'db', 'CacheTableName' => 'pradocache'],
    ],
],
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<h3>TMemCache</h3>
<p class="block-content">
Stores entries in a memcached server, shared across the machines that connect to it and
lost when it restarts. It needs the <tt>memcached</tt> extension and raises a
<tt>TConfigurationException</tt> when that is missing. <tt>Host</tt> and <tt>Port</tt> name
the server, <tt>PersistentID</tt> reuses a connection between requests, and
<tt>Options</tt> passes client options through. More than one server is declared as
<tt>&lt;server&gt;</tt> elements inside the module. The module reads that list from the XML
form only, so an application on the PHP form names a single server through <tt>Host</tt>
and <tt>Port</tt>.
</p>

<h3>TRedisCache</h3>
<com:SinceVersion Version="4.0" />
<p class="block-content">
Stores entries in Redis. It needs the <tt>redis</tt> extension and raises a
<tt>TConfigurationException</tt> without it. <tt>Host</tt> and <tt>Port</tt> name the
server, <tt>Socket</tt> connects over a unix socket instead, and <tt>Index</tt> selects the
numbered database within the server.
</p>

<h3>TEtcdCache</h3>
<com:SinceVersion Version="4.0" />
<p class="block-content">
Stores entries in an etcd key-value store over its HTTP interface, which suits a
distributed deployment that already runs one. <tt>Host</tt> and <tt>Port</tt> name the
server and <tt>Dir</tt> is the key directory the module writes under.
</p>

<h3>TAPCCache</h3>
<p class="block-content">
Stores entries in the APC user cache, in the memory of the PHP process itself. It is the
fastest of the five and the least shareable: entries belong to one server, and in some
deployments to one process pool. It needs the <tt>apcu</tt> extension.
</p>

<h2>Dependencies</h2>

<p class="block-content">
An expiry time says how long a value may be stale. A dependency says what makes it stale.
Passing one to <tt>set</tt> or <tt>add</tt> records it alongside the value, and the cache
checks it on every read: when the dependency has changed, the read returns false as though
the entry had expired.
</p>

<com:TTextHighlighter Language="php" CssClass="source block-content">
use Prado\Caching\TFileCacheDependency;

$cache-&gt;set('parsed.rates', $rates, 0,
    new TFileCacheDependency($this-&gt;Application-&gt;BasePath . '/data/rates.xml'));
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<p class="block-content">
That entry never expires on time. It is discarded when, and only when, the file it was
built from changes.
</p>

<table class="parameters">
<tr><th>Dependency</th><th>Reports a change when</th></tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>TFileCacheDependency</tt></td>
	<td>The file named by <tt>FileName</tt> has a new modification time.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>TDirectoryCacheDependency</tt></td>
	<td>Any file under <tt>Directory</tt> changes. <tt>RecursiveCheck</tt> decides whether subdirectories count, and <tt>RecursiveLevel</tt> how deep to look.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>TGlobalStateCacheDependency</tt></td>
	<td>The <a href="?page=Advanced.State">global state</a> named by <tt>StateName</tt> changes.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>TApplicationStateCacheDependency</tt></td>
	<td>The application is not running in performance mode. Chain it so that caching only holds firm once the application is deployed.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>TChainedCacheDependency</tt></td>
	<td>Any dependency in its <tt>Dependencies</tt> list reports a change.</td>
</tr>
</table>

<p class="block-content">
A dependency of your own implements <tt>ICacheDependency</tt>. Checking happens on every
read of the entry, so keep the check cheap: a directory dependency over a large tree costs
more than the value it protects.
</p>

<h2>Caching part of a page</h2>

<p class="block-content">
<a href="?page=Controls.OutputCache">TOutputCache</a> caches the rendered output of a
region of a page rather than a value, and the controls inside a cached region are not
created at all. It uses whichever cache module is configured, and has its own key
variation and dependency handling.
</p>

<p class="block-content">
<a href="?page=Advanced.Performance">Performance tuning</a> covers where caching fits
among the other ways to make an application faster.
</p>

</com:TContent>
